INT. BOTANICAL GARDEN ORCHID HOUSE - MORNING

Humid green dim. Mist beading on glass. A path of wet flagstones between banks of blooms.

DETECTIVE SOLOMON OKAFOR-LINDQVIST (50s, trench coat, notebook) crouches beside an empty planter. A tag reads PHALAENOPSIS "GHOST". Nothing grows there.

CURATOR AMELIA DRUMMOND-ADEBAYO (40s, cardigan, trowel in her back pocket) stands over him, arms crossed.

SOLOMON
When did you last see it?

AMELIA
Last Thursday. Or last Wednesday.

SOLOMON
Which?

AMELIA
I water them in sequence. Thursday is the third bench.

SOLOMON
So Thursday.

AMELIA
So Thursday.

Footsteps on the gravel. A young man comes round the corner at a trot, apron flapping. A volunteer: THADDEUS BROUGHAM-ASANTE (20s, name tag on a lanyard). He freezes when he sees the detective.

THADDEUS
Sorry. I didn't know.

AMELIA
Thaddeus. Go back to the lilies.

THADDEUS
Yes, ma'am.

He does not move. Amelia does not look at him. Solomon, still crouched, studies Thaddeus's hands. They are stained with potting soil, and a single white petal is stuck to his wrist.

SOLOMON
Thaddeus.

THADDEUS
Sir.

AMELIA ^
Thaddeus.

Their voices collide on the name. Thaddeus swallows. He peels the petal off his wrist and holds it out on his open palm.

THADDEUS (CONT'D)
It was dying.

Amelia says nothing. Solomon straightens slowly, closing his notebook, and in the green dim the mist begins, gently, to settle.
